window.VGRE_SEARCH_INDEX = [{"url": "index.html", "title": "Introduction", "section": "Overview", "text": "VGRE — Virtual GPU RuntimeRun unmodified CUDA on any CPU — no GPU required. Plus a full local-AI stack: train, fine-tune with LoRA, retrieve with an in-tree vector index, and generate — all offline, all dependency-free.Linux CI-greenmacOS CI-greenWindows CI-greenLLVM-free buildCUDA ~95%Zero vendor lock-inVGRE intercepts CUDA and OpenCL API calls and executes kernels on the CPU. It does not need LLVM to run kernels: a from-scratch CUDA-C front-end (own lexer/parser → AST) feeds a four-tier CPU backend — a PTX interpreter, a compiled-closure tier with a cooperative fiber executor (__shared__ / _"}, {"url": "quickstart.html", "title": "Quick Start", "section": "Overview", "text": "Quick StartFrom a one-line install to a running CUDA kernel on CPU in minutes.Fastest: install the prebuilt wheel (no toolchain)Each GitHub Release ships a self-contained, LLVM-free wheel per platform (Linux / macOS / Windows). It bundles the native engine, so you need nothing but Python 3.8+ and NumPy — no compiler, no CUDA, no GPU:# Grab the wheel for your platform from the latest release, then: pip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whl # or -macosx_* / -win_amd64 python -c &quot;import vgre; print(&#x27;native:&#x27;, vgre.NATIVE_AVAILABLE)&quot;TipReleases: github.com/etienne0114/vg"}, {"url": "downloads.html", "title": "Downloads", "section": "Overview", "text": "DownloadsPrebuilt, self-contained wheels — no compiler, no CUDA, no GPU.Every GitHub Release ships a self-contained, LLVM-free wheel per platform. Each bundles the native engine, so you need only Python 3.8+ and NumPy. The wheels are built and smoke-tested (import + train a language model) on Linux, macOS and Windows runners in CI.Latest release — v0.1.0PlatformWheelInstallLinux x86-64vgre-0.1.0-…-linux_x86_64.whlpip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-linux_x86_64.whlmacOS (Apple Silicon)vgre-0.1.0-…-macosx_universal2.whlpip install vgre-0.1.0-py3-none-macosx_10_13_universal2.whlWindows x86-64vgre-0."}, {"url": "installation.html", "title": "Installation", "section": "Overview", "text": "InstallationManual build steps and platform notes.Prerequisites# Ubuntu / Debian sudo apt install -y cmake ninja-build clang llvm-18-dev \\ libomp-dev libssl-dev libsqlite3-dev # macOS (Homebrew — paths discovered automatically at configure time) brew install cmake llvm@18 libomp ninja sqlite # Flutter (for the dashboard) — one of: sudo snap install flutter --classic # or: brew install --cask flutterBuild (full, with LLVM JIT)cmake -S . -B build -G Ninja -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release cmake --build build -j$(nproc) ctest --test-dir build -j$(nproc) # full suite: 394/394 on LinuxZero-burden build (n"}, {"url": "running-cuda.html", "title": "Running CUDA Apps", "section": "Overview", "text": "Running CUDA ApplicationsHow VGRE intercepts and executes CUDA on the CPU.Execution modelVGRE parses CUDA-C kernels through a Clang AST, lowers them to LLVM IR, and JIT-compiles to native code with -O3 -march=native. Kernel grids run on an OpenMP thread pool with SIMD vectorization; managed memory is backed by an OS page-fault handler.What worksCUDA Runtime API (~101 functions): memory, streams, events, graphs, textures/surfaces, cooperative launch, CDP.CUDA Driver API (~56 functions): contexts, modules, PTX load + JIT link.cuBLAS (L1/L2/L3, complex), cuDNN (conv/pool/norm/attention/RNN), cuFF"}, {"url": "cluster.html", "title": "Distributed Clusters", "section": "Overview", "text": "Distributed ClustersScale kernels across machines over LAN or WAN.Token setupEvery node in a cluster shares one auth token. Generate it on the master and distribute it to workers:# On the MASTER vgre-token generate # writes ~/.vgre/token, prints SHA-256 fingerprint # On every WORKER (choose one) scp master:~/.vgre/token ~/.vgre/token vgre-token set &lt;paste-token-here&gt; # Verify both sides match vgre-token fingerprintStart the cluster# Master (LAN auto-discovery on) vgre-start --master # Workers on the same LAN vgre-start --worker # Workers over WAN (explicit master address) vgre-start --wo"}, {"url": "dashboard.html", "title": "The Dashboard", "section": "Overview", "text": "The DashboardA real-time Flutter monitor for the running engine.Launch with vgre-dashboard. It loads the native library in-process and polls live telemetry — no mock data. Pages:PageWhat it showsDashboardLive utilization gauges, workload throughput, UVM activity, log stream.Kernel ExplorerJIT-compiled kernels, launch counts, occupancy, per-kernel timing.Cluster TopologyConnected nodes, per-node load, network heatmap.Hardware TuningToggle background compute, block-threads, profiler; set cache model.Memory AnalysisAllocation map, bandwidth stats, UVM page residency.SettingsPolling interval, data"}, {"url": "local-ai.html", "title": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LM", "section": "Overview", "text": "Local AI: LoRA · RAG · LMA complete offline machine-learning stack built into the runtime — no PyTorch, no FAISS, no external tokenizer library.LoRA fine-tuningvgre.nn.LoRALinear adds trainable low-rank adapters over a frozen base weight, so you can fine-tune on a CPU/laptop with a fraction of the parameters. Adapters merge back to the base for zero-overhead inference.from vgre import nn layer = nn.LoRALinear.from_linear(pretrained, r=8, alpha=16) opt = nn.AdamW(layer.adapter_parameters(), lr=1e-3) # trains A,B only # ... training loop ... layer.merge() # fold adapter into W for fast inference"}, {"url": "debugging.html", "title": "Debugging PTX (GDB)", "section": "Overview", "text": "Debugging PTX with GDBCUDA-GDB-style single-stepping through PTX kernels using a stock gdb — no GPU, no cuda-gdb install.How it worksVGRE ships a PTX single-step interpreter and a GDB remote-serial-protocol server. Each CUDA thread appears as a GDB thread (a lane); the kernel's own virtual PTX registers are exposed to info registers; and breakpoints, stepi, and memory inspection all behave normally in an unmodified gdb.Start the stubvgre_ptx_gdbserver kernel.ptx saxpy 1 4 2000 \\ f32:2.0 buf:f32:4=1,2,3,4 buf:f32:4=10,20,30,40 u32:4 # prints the instruction listing, buffer addresses, and: # gdb"}, {"url": "env-vars.html", "title": "Environment Variables", "section": "Overview", "text": "Environment VariablesWritten to ~/.vgre/env by the installer and loaded automatically.CoreVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_LIB_PATHautoAbsolute path to the shared library.VGRE_LOG_LEVELINFODEBUG | INFO | WARN | ERROR.VGRE_INSTALL_DIR~/.local/share/VGREInstallation directory.VGRE_WORKER_THREADSnprocOverride worker thread count.VGRE_SIMD_LEVELautoForce SSE4 | AVX | AVX2 | AVX512.VGRE_ENABLE_NUMA0NUMA-aware scheduling (Linux).Execution backendVariableDefaultDescriptionVGRE_EXEC_BACKENDautoSelect the execution tier: interp (PTX interpreter), cp (compiled-fiber), or ssa (the Tier-2 SSA optimizing bac"}, {"url": "api.html", "title": "API Reference", "section": "Overview", "text": "API ReferencePython, C ABI, and the CUDA-runtime shim.Python bindingsThe vgre package wraps the native library via ctypes:SymbolPurposevgre.TokenizerBPE tokenizer; train, load_hf, encode, decode.vgre.LanguageModelIn-tree GPT; train, generate, save, load.vgre.nn.LoRALinearLow-rank adapters; adapter_parameters, merge.vgre.vector.VectorIndexHNSW ANN; add, search, save, load.C ABI (vgre_c_api.h)The stable C surface the wheel and dashboard bind to. Highlights:vgre_init(); vgre_set_profiler_enabled(1); vgre_set_block_threads(0); vgre_get_memory_bandwidth_stats(&amp;stats); // tokenizer vgre_bpe* t ="}, {"url": "cli.html", "title": "CLI Tools", "section": "Overview", "text": "CLI ToolsThe command-line utilities installed with VGRE.CommandPurposevgre-dashboardLaunch the real-time Flutter monitor.vgre-startStart a master or worker node (--master / --worker / --test).vgre-tokenGenerate / set / fingerprint the shared cluster auth token.vgre-discoverPublic-IP discovery and cross-LAN registration.vgre_ptx_gdbserverServe a PTX kernel to a stock gdb for step debugging.gpt2_inferReal GPT-2 inference from safetensors/GGUF (matches Hugging Face).Examplesvgre-start --test # local self-test vgre-token generate # new token + fingerprint vgre-discover --set-master # publish publi"}, {"url": "architecture.html", "title": "Architecture", "section": "Overview", "text": "ArchitectureHow the pieces fit together.LayersInterception — CUDA/OpenCL API shims capture calls at load time (a drop-in libcudart preload, or the C ABI / Python bindings).Front-end — a from-scratch CUDA-C lexer/parser lowers kernels to an AST (no Clang required); an optional Clang/LLVM path is used when LLVM dev libs are present.Execution (four CPU tiers, fastest-first, bit-exact) — see the table below.Memory — UVM via an OS page-fault handler; stream-ordered pools; LZ4-compressed cluster transfers.Distribution — authenticated, encrypted TCP transport; NCCL-style + software-RDMA collectives; "}, {"url": "faq.html", "title": "FAQ & Troubleshooting", "section": "Overview", "text": "FAQ &amp; TroubleshootingDoes VGRE need a GPU?No. That is the point — kernels execute on the CPU. A GPU is never required.Is it as fast as a real GPU?No. Expect 10–50× slower for compute-bound kernels; memory-bound kernels are closer with AVX-512. VGRE targets learning, CI, development, and moderate workloads — not latency-critical production inference.Which platforms are supported?Linux x86-64/ARM64 is fully verified (full ctest green). macOS (Apple Silicon and Intel) is build-verified: the native engine compiles with auto-detected Homebrew llvm@18, runs JIT kernels, and passes integration te"}];
